      title: `3. Minimally Invasive & Robotic Revascularization Architecture`,
      content: `**Minimally Invasive Direct Coronary Artery Bypass (MIDCAB):** Completed via a small left anterior thoracotomy to graft the LIMA directly to the LAD. It requires **Single-Lung Ventilation (SLV)** using a double-lumen endotracheal tube or a bronchial blocker to deflate the left lung and expose the LIMA. External pacing/defibrillation pads must be placed pre-induction, as direct surgical access to the heart is restricted through the thoracotomy window. 

**Total Endoscopic Coronary Artery Bypass (TECAB):** A completely closed, port-access robotic revascularization technique. It can be performed on an arrested heart using a **Remotely Accessed Perfusion Technique**. An **Endoaortic Occlusion Balloon Clamp (EAOBC)** is advanced retrograde through the femoral artery into the ascending aorta to provide internal occlusion and cardioplegia delivery. Real-time TEE monitoring is required to guide balloon placement, confirm stable positioning, and monitor for dangerous balloon migration. Distal migration can occlude the brachiocephalic trunk, while proximal migration can cause severe aortic regurgitation or failure of cardioplegia delivery.**`
